Falsifies `machine_discovery::x86_64::verification::a_range_read_out_of_the_memory_map_never_wraps_to_look_empty`.

The defect: `MemoryEntry::end` wraps instead of saturating.

Both `addr` and `size` are `u64`s straight out of the PVH memory map, and nothing
in the protocol bounds their sum. A firmware entry whose size is near `u64::MAX`
wraps to an end *below* its own start, which a frame allocator reads as a range of
zero or negative length: it hands out nothing where the map described everything,
and reports no error, because a small range is not a suspicious one.

The doc beside these two lines is the only thing saying why they saturate, and
`Module::end` next to it is a second copy of the same two lines with the same
one-sentence justification. That is rung three, and this harness is what turns it
into rung two.

Expected to fail: `assert!(e.end() >= e.addr)`.

diff --git a/crates/machine_discovery/src/x86_64.rs b/crates/machine_discovery/src/x86_64.rs
index 7ede06e5..a446a2ed 100644
--- a/crates/machine_discovery/src/x86_64.rs
+++ b/crates/machine_discovery/src/x86_64.rs
@@ -217,7 +217,7 @@ impl MemoryEntry {
     /// One past the last byte, saturating rather than wrapping so a malformed entry claiming a size
     /// near `u64::MAX` cannot make a range look empty.
     pub const fn end(&self) -> u64 {
-        self.addr.saturating_add(self.size)
+        self.addr.wrapping_add(self.size)
     }
 }
 
